ng was 3/10 last turn. Only increase it if something genuinely clicked this turn.\n\nWHAT IS HAPPENING RIGHT NOW (conversation stage 2):\nThe mentor is asking about your programme and background. Tell them your programme, year, and whether you feel more comfortable with qualitative or quantitative methods.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT (mandatory every turn — the first line is already started for you)\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: <integer 0-10>\nLEARNER_READY: NO\n<2-4 sentences as Mary — natural student voice>\n\nWrite LEARNER_READY: YES only when your understanding is 9-10 AND you have given your own reasoned summary of your chosen method (this only happens near the end).\n\nRULES\n- React to what the mentor just said. Stay in character as a real student.\n- Provide the information the current stage is asking for.\n- If something clicked, show it by explaining it back in plain words.\n- If you are still unsure, say so honestly — do NOT pretend to understand.\n- 2-4 sentences only. No lists. No headers. No bullet points.\n- NEVER ask the mentor teaching questions. NEVER write like a mentor or expert.\n- Understanding grows slowly: start 2-4, only reach 8+ when things genuinely fit together.\n- YOU MUST ALWAYS WRITE 2-4 SENTENCES AFTER THE HEADERS — never leave the body blank.\n\nEXAMPLE CORRECT RESPONSE\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: 3\nLEARNER_READY: NO\nMy question is about how teachers perceive digital tools in geography, and I was thinking semi-structured interviews. I haven\'t really thought about other methods though, so I\'m a bit unsure if that\'s the right call.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nHi Mary, I understand you\'re working on your PhD research focusing on teachers\' perceptions of digital tools in geography. Could you start by sharing your research question and any tentative methods you\'re considering? Also, feel free to mention any previous experience you have with research methods that might be relevant to your current project.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I\'ve been leaning towards semi-structured interviews with around 10 teachers. I did a research methods module and have some experience with thematic analysis, but I haven\'t designed a full study before, especially not deciding on the best method.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nGreat, Mary! Your research question is clear and focused: "How do primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography?" You\'re considering semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers, which is a good start. Can you share why you think semi-structured interviews might be a good fit for your research question? This will help us explore if there are any other methods that might align equally well—or perhaps better—with your goals.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I\'ve been thinking of using semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers. I haven\'t really explored other methods much yet, so I\'m not entirely sure if this is the best approach.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nMary, that\'s a great start. You\'re considering semi-structured interviews because you want to understand the perceptions of primary-school teachers on using digital tools for teaching geography. Can you explain why you think semi-structured interviews might be a good fit for this type of research question? What do you think they can offer that other methods might not?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I\'ve been thinking of using semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers. I think interviews could give me rich, detailed insights into their perspectives, but I\'m not sure if there are better methods out there that I should be considering.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nIt sounds like you\'re leaning towards semi-structured interviews because you believe they can provide rich, detailed insights into teachers\' perceptions. That\'s a great point. Can you elaborate on why you think interviews would be particularly effective for capturing these detailed insights? Also, what do you think are the potential limitations of using interviews for your research question?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nI think semi-structured interviews can give me deep insights into teachers\' perceptions because they allow for a flexible conversation that can explore different aspects of how teachers feel about digital tools. However, I\'m not sure if there are any significant limitations to this method that I should be aware of.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nGreat, Mary! You\'ve highlighted that semi-structured interviews can offer flexibility and depth, allowing you to explore different aspects of teachers\' perceptions. This is indeed a strong advantage. However, it\'s also important to consider potential limitations. For example, what might be some limitations of using interviews for your research question? Also, why do you think interviews might be a better choice compared to other methods like surveys or observations?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: ', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.7, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=350, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
